  'ck.plus': 'Counting view: with the cards left in this shoe, {b} has a positive expectation — a rare spot, usually for side bets late in a shoe.',
  'tips.h': 'Things to think about', 'tips.more': 'More tips ({n})', 'tips.less': 'Fewer',
  'tip.highEdge': '{n} carries a big house edge. Keep it small, just for fun.',
  'tip.bot': '{n} doubles the bet after every loss (now {a}) — a Martingale. See where it leads in Study → Betting systems.', 'tip.bigBet': 'This coup is about {n}% of your bankroll. A usual guide is 1–5% per bet.',
  'tip.both': 'Betting both Player and Banker means one always loses — you just pay the house edge.',
  'tip.streak': '{w} has won {n} in a row. The next coup\'s odds are still the same (Banker ≈45.9%, Player ≈44.6%).',
  'tip.chop': 'Lately the winner keeps switching (a "chop"). A road shows the past, not the future.',
  'tip.luck': 'Expected loss so far ≈ {e}; your actual net is {n}. The difference is luck — it shrinks as you play more.',
  'tipg.1': 'Banker wins about 45.9%, Player 44.6%, Tie 9.5% (eight decks).',
  'tipg.2': 'Every coup is independent — the last result does not change the next one\'s odds (the gambler\'s fallacy).',
  'tipg.3': 'On a Tie, bets on Player and Banker are returned (a push, not a loss).',
  'tipg.4': 'An 8 or 9 with the first two cards is a "natural". If either side has one, nobody draws.',
  'tipg.5': 'The 5% commission is taken only when Banker wins — never on a loss.',
  'tipg.6': 'A pair comes about 7.5% of the time (eight decks). Even at 11 to 1 the house edge is about 10.4%.',
  'tipg.7': 'Average loss = house edge × total wagered. The more you play, the bigger the expected loss.',
  'tipg.8': 'Systems like the Martingale (double after a loss) do not change the house edge — they just make the rare big loss bigger. Try them in Study → Betting systems.',
  'tipg.9': 'Deciding beforehand how much you may lose is the money management that works best.',
  'tipg.10': 'Card counting barely helps the main bets in baccarat. Side bets can turn favourable late in a shoe.',
  'tipg.11': 'Big Road: red = Banker, blue = Player. A new column starts when the winning side changes.',
  'tipg.12': 'Red and blue in Big Eye Boy, Small Road and Cockroach Pig mean "regular" and "choppy" — not Banker and Player.',
  'tipg.13': 'The Bead Plate records every result in order, ties included.',
  'tipg.14': 'Streaks of six or more (a "dragon") are not rare within one shoe — long columns are normal.',
  'tipg.15': 'Peeling the cards slowly ("the squeeze") is a ritual loved by high rollers in Macau and Asia. It does not change the result.',
  'tipg.16': 'Player draws on 0–5 and stands on 6–7. The rules decide — there is no choice to make.',
  'tipg.17': 'Whether Banker draws depends on Banker\'s total and Player\'s third card (see Study → Drawing rules).',
  'tipg.18': 'At the start of a shoe one card is turned and that many are burned. A cut card sits near the end.',
  'tipg.19': 'A Tie paying 8 to 1 has a house edge of about 14.4%; at 9 to 1 about 4.8%. Table rules matter.',
  'tipg.20': 'Banker and Player (about 1.06% and 1.24%) are among the lowest house edges in the casino.',
  'tipg.21': 'Only the last digit counts; 10, J, Q, K are worth 0. A hand of zeros is "baccarat" — the name is said to mean zero.',
  'tipg.22': 'Compare "expected loss" with your actual net in the stats: the gap is how lucky you have been.',
  'tipg.23': 'Once the bets are down there is nothing to decide. The skill is where to bet, how much, and when to stop.',
  'tipg.24': 'Flat betting — the same amount on the same side — gives the smallest swings.',
  'tipv.classic': 'In Punto Banco a 5% commission is taken from Banker wins (bet 100, win 95).',
  'tipv.nocomm': 'In Super 6 a Banker win with a 6 pays half. That happens about once in 19 coups.',
  'tipv.ez1': 'In EZ Baccarat a Banker win with a three-card 7 pushes — about once in 44 coups. That replaces the commission.',
  'tipv.ez2': 'Dragon 7 (40 to 1) has a house edge of about 7.6%, Panda 8 (25 to 1) about 10.2%. Late in some shoes they turn favourable.',
