  'x.natural': 'If the first two cards make 8 or 9, it is a "natural". If either side has one, nobody draws — the coup is decided.\nIf both are naturals, the higher wins (equal is a tie).',
  'tip.natural': 'A natural 9 is the best hand. 9 beats 8.',
  'x.pdraw': 'Without a natural, Player goes first: on 0–5 Player draws a third card, on 6–7 Player stands.\nThe rules decide — the bettor never chooses.',
  'tip.pdraw': 'Just remember: five or less, draw.',
  'x.bdraw': 'If Player stood, Banker also draws on 0–5 and stands on 6–7.\nIf Player drew, Banker\'s total and Player\'s third card decide: 0–2 always draw · 3 draws unless the third card is 8 · 4 draws on 2–7 · 5 on 4–7 · 6 on 6–7 · 7 stands.',
  'tip.bdraw': 'The higher Banker\'s total, the fewer cases in which Banker draws.',
  'x.winner': 'After the third cards, the total (last digit) closest to 9 wins. Equal totals are a tie.',
  'tip.winner': 'The number of cards does not matter — only the totals.',
  'x.payout': 'A win returns your stake plus the payout. Player pays 1 to 1 (100 → 200), Banker 0.95 to 1 after the 5% commission (100 → 195), Tie 8 to 1 (100 → 900), a pair 11 to 1 (100 → 1,200).',
  'tip.payout': '"What comes back" = stake + payout.',
  'x.edge': 'The house edge is the share you lose per bet on average (eight decks): Banker ≈1.06%, Player ≈1.24%, Tie (8 to 1) ≈14.4%, pairs ≈10.4%.\nIn no-commission Super 6 Banker rises to ≈1.46%, so Player becomes the better bet.',
  'tip.edge': 'The smaller the edge, the less you lose over a long session.',
  'x.tie': 'Equal totals are a Tie. Bets on Player and Banker are not lost — they come back.\nA Tie bet pays 8 to 1 (9 to 1 at some tables).',
  'tip.tie': 'Ties come about 9.5% of the time — 8 to 1 is not enough to make the bet fair.',
  'x.variants': 'Super 6 (no commission): a Banker win with a 6 pays half, every other Banker win pays 1 to 1.\nEZ Baccarat: a Banker win with a three-card 7 pushes. Side bets: Dragon 7 (that Banker hand) pays 40 to 1, Panda 8 (Player wins with a three-card 8) 25 to 1.',
  'tip.variants': 'No commission — the house takes its share somewhere else.',
  'x.bead': 'The Bead Plate lists every result in order: six down from the top left, then the next column. Red = Banker, blue = Player, green = Tie.',
  'tip.bead': 'Count by colour. Unlike the other roads, ties are included.',
  'x.bigroad': 'In the Big Road the same side keeps going down the same column; when the winner changes, write at the top of a new column to the right. A tie makes no column — it is a green slash on the last circle.',
  'tip.bigroad': 'The length of a column = the length of that streak.',
  'x.derived': 'Big Eye Boy gives every new Big Road circle a red or blue mark.\nFirst circle of a new column: red if the previous column and the one before it are equally long, otherwise blue.\nFurther down a column: look one column back — a circle in the same row → red; none there but one in the row above → blue; neither → red.',
  'tip.derived': 'Red and blue mean "regular" or not — not Banker and Player.',
  'x.meaning': 'Roads organise past results so they are easy to read. Casinos love them and they make good conversation — but every coup is independent, so they cannot predict the next one.',
  'tip.meaning': 'Enjoy the roads, but decide your bet sizes by rules.',
  'x.indep': 'The shoe is well shuffled; a result does not affect the next one (strictly, the remaining cards shift the odds a tiny bit — very little).\nThinking "the other side is due" is the gambler\'s fallacy.',
  'tip.indep': 'Streaks, good and bad, happen just as probability says.',
  'x.system': 'The Martingale doubles the bet after each loss. One win recovers everything plus one base bet — but a losing run grows the bet 2, 4, 8, 16… very fast.\nNo system changes the house edge on the money you bet.',
  'tip.system': 'A "winning system" only changes the shape of your results.',
  'x.loss': 'Average loss (expected loss) = house edge × total wagered. 100 on Banker 100 times: 10,000 wagered, about 106 lost on average.',
